            <ArticleTitle>Analgesic effectiveness of subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations as an adjunct treatment in patients with non-specific neck or low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>68-76</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the analgesic effectiveness of subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations in addition to standard physical treatment in patients with non-specific neck or low back pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A pragmatic, randomized controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rehabilitation hospital inpatients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients received either subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations (10 treatments) and standard physical treatment or standard physical treatment only.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Affective pain perception (42-point scale), sensory pain perception (30-point scale), pain intensity (100 mm visual analogue scale).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Between-groups differences were -2.2 [95% CI -5.2; +0.9] (affective pain perception), -1.2 [-3, 0; + 0.7] (sensory pain perception), and -6.5 [-14; +1.0] (pain intensity) respectively in favour of subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations do not seem to be a worthwhile adjunct in the given setting of inpatient rehabilitation. Trials in a monotherapeutic setting, which aim more at the efficacy of subcutaneous carbon-dioxide insufflations, might help to solve this issue.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
